\begin{proof}
For each $n\in\mathbb N$, let $A_n$ be a countable dense subset of $X_n$, and choose a point $x_n\in X_n$. Let $A$ consist of all sequences $y=(y_n)\in X=\prod_nX_n$ such that $y_n\in A_n$ for only finitely many coordinates and $y_n=x_n$ at every other coordinate. Equivalently,
\[
A=\bigcup_{F\subseteq\mathbb N,\,F\text{ finite}}\prod_n B_n^F,
\qquad B_n^F=\begin{cases}A_n,&n\in F,\\\{x_n\},&n\notin F.\end{cases}
\]
For each finite $F$, the set $\prod_nB_n^F$ is countable, since it is determined by the finitely many coordinates in $F$. Since $\mathbb N$ has only countably many finite subsets, $A$ is countable.

To prove density, take any basic open set $V=\prod_nV_n$ in $X$. Let $F=\{n:V_n\ne X_n\}$, which is finite. If $V$ is nonempty, each $V_n$ with $n\in F$ is nonempty; therefore, because $A_n$ is dense, choose $y_n\in A_n\cap V_n$. For $n\notin F$, put $y_n=x_n$. Then $y\in A\cap V$. Thus every nonempty basic open set meets $A$, and $A$ is dense in $X$.
\end{proof}
